# GitHub Actions — standard runners only

Never use, enable, recommend, or configure **GitHub-hosted larger runners**. Absolute — billing risk and Free-account blocks do not get exceptions.

## Forbidden

- Org/enterprise **larger runners** (any size) and runner groups that exist only to expose them
- Workflow `runs-on` labels that select larger runners (e.g. `ubuntu-latest-4-cores`, `macos-latest-xl`, custom larger-runner labels)
- Docs, comments, or PR notes that tell humans to enable larger runners in GitHub settings
- Switching a public-repo job to a paid larger SKU “for speed”

```yaml
# ❌ BAD — larger / paid hosted SKUs
runs-on: ubuntu-latest-4-cores
runs-on: macos-latest-xl

# ✅ GOOD — standard GitHub-hosted runners
runs-on: ubuntu-latest
runs-on: macos-latest
runs-on: macos-14
```

## Allowed

- Standard GitHub-hosted labels only: `ubuntu-latest`, `windows-latest`, `macos-latest`, and versioned standard images (`ubuntu-24.04`, `macos-14`, …)
- Self-hosted runners the team already operates (unrelated to GitHub’s larger-runner product)
- Keeping existing workflows on `ubuntu-latest` unless a task explicitly needs macOS for iOS/Xcode — still use **standard** `macos-*` only

## If a job is too slow or OOMs

Do not “fix” it with a larger runner. Shrink the job, split steps, cache, or ask a human. Leave CI on standard runners.
